<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peking University</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as PKU, Imperial University of Peking, National University of Peking</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peking University is a public university in Haidian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, founded in 1898 as the Imperial University of Peking by royal charter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform</a>; it is the second-oldest university in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tianjin</a> University. Renamed in 1912, it was transformed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cai-yuanpei">Cai Yuanpei</a>, president from 1917, into the country's largest institution of higher learning, with a faculty that included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih">Hu Shih</a>, Li Dazhao, Chen Duxiu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lu-xun">Lu Xun</a>. Its students helped lead the protest of 4 May 1919 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>. In 1952 it merged with Yenching University.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peking-university.jpg" alt="Peking University" width="330" height="219" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: galaygobi · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PekingUniversityPic6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On the afternoon of May 4 over 4,000 students of Yenching University, <b>Peking University</b> and other schools gathered in front of Tiananmen.</blockquote><a href="/movement/may-fourth-movement">May Fourth Movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>A Chinese girl and <b>Peking University</b> students sang it at the closing ceremony of the 2004 Athens Olympics to introduce Beijing, and an adaptation by Tan Dun and Wang Hesheng, chosen from more than 4,000 pieces, opened the victory theme at the medal ceremonies of the Beijing 2008…</blockquote><a href="/songs/mo-li-hua">Mo Li Hua</a></li></ul></section>
